the question for each of you is perhaps  especially when you're portraying an  actor that you have someone who's a real  person versus a character that whom you  can really build from the ground up how  different is that process well in the  case of Gloria of course I got to watch  her films and having all of those great  movies and some not so great which was  also fascinating to watch and to watch  how she evolved and so that was actually  really helpful but of course I also felt  a great responsibility that I didn't  want to do a caricature or an imitation  of her but also I wanted to feel like  you had a sense of oh yeah that's that  woman that was in those films but she's  at a different stage of her life but  there once she stopped making movies she  also did quite a bit of television and  she made some not-so-great movies I  watched those two so that was very  helpful but knowing you know you fall in  love with a character when you play them  you want to do I wanted to do her  justice and I wanted to also do the  story justice what's so beautiful about  the book is how impressionistic it is  and economical it is and he actually  leaves most things out it's not a  biography at all so you don't really  hear about her life you don't hear about  her films or her complicated personal  life which she had had before she met  Peter it's so tasteful it's a beautiful  and just absolutely like you said  unbelievable story the fact that she  would end up in Liverpool with this  family  the out-of-sequence storytelling seemed  like it was a coil that was getting  tighter and tighter to bring about that  dramatic dynamo is that what it felt  like as you were doing all of those  scenes and moving back and forth in time  I think that when the movie is as the  movie is the relationship you know so  trying to chart the trajectory of how  you're gonna play all those scenes and  hoping that we're getting the you know  getting the gray areas of a relationship  is and all those scenes across of course  the shooting schedule doesn't care about  any of that stuff so we literally was  saying goodbye in the taxi on day 2  of the shoe I was just you know it's  mental I think we were doing the Romeo  and Juliet scene on day four or five you  know that's supposed to have that scene  is supposed to span a three and a half  years of them being together of you know  falling in love and breaking apart and  not seeing each other in that now  learning she's sick all of that you know  you have to try and prepare I mean I  suppose that's the access job but it was  very challenging I mean I love what Paul  McGuigan our director brought to this in  terms of his the theatricality of  elevating the material using glorious  films I'll be kind of abstractly and  paying homage to some of those movies  with all the back projection that you  see in physical sets that rotated and  moved around and we moved from one you  know year to another year or one  location to another and all that stuff I  think it's really heightened the story  and added to it give it's kind of  cinematic touch but it was certainly  challenging what was Paul McGuigan like  as a director was that the loose reins  approach and or was he he was very loose  friend  yeah I mean I think he trusted his  actors which is that's the thrill of  working is when you feel that you trust  the director they trust you and you're  willing to test each other and so much  of the scenes me and Annette kind of  going toe-to-toe with one another and he  just kind of let us go and do it he and  I he I know we were with him recently  and he was talking about it so he  certainly said that a number of times  that what he was doing he was watching  us and let us do letting us do what we  wanted to do  having said that he also had a very  specific way he approached the story and  the style of the movie which I really  like it's not a naturalistic movie it  has a kind of heightened reality to it  and that was Paul's idea it was his idea  to have that scene where you see it from  two different points of view the big  fight and when they break up and all of  that that was that was very much Paul's  idea and his approach so but from an  acting standpoint totally he really let  us let us do what you know what we  wanted to do and we felt very free and  he's also I mean I really like  his taste I like the way he uses the  camera you don't feel self doesn't the  camera doesn't call attention to itself  the way he's showing you what's  happening he's I just like that approach  and also very often he's not explicit in  what in the way he's shooting which I  also I like that a little bit about the  dynamic with the family with Julie  Walters and you know the the the  screaming angry brother who I a couple  of times I had a hard time understanding  I thought live the Liverpudlian of the  Beatles is long out of my head so I had  to learn how John Lennon talks all over  again well the first thing that I've  been reunited with Julie waters since  our first movie that we did together all  those years ago so it was very special  for me to to get to work with her again  I didn't know just how beloved she is I  mean I said I certainly I did but I I  work with her as a kid i that wasn't the  kind of thing I was really paying  attention to trying to figure out how to  be an actor I still and most of the time  but but uh but this experience with  those 17 years lead and now walking onto  the set just seeing how the entire crew  would they relate to her like she is a  relative of this she puts everyone at  immediate comfort she can crack a joke  and then do a serious scene and a  heartbeat  you know she's wonderful also when we  got the bunch of those actors together  Julie Steven and Kenneth who plays my  father their scenes just came alive it's  so easy there were the scenes that we  just didn't need to worry about it was  just everyone knew right it's  working-class England kitchen sink scene  and we're all kind of really good at  that because we're all from those kinds  of places and from those kinds of towns  and those were some of the easiest  things most fun scenes to shoot by the  way it's Stephen Graham who plays my  brother mostly known for being tough guy  actor playing very kind of criminal type  parts reduced to a nervous schoolboy the  day that he met Julie Walters for the  first time she has that effect on people  you talked about the sense of  responsibility to Gloria Grahame and and  what this film conveyed the fragility  and the vulnerability but the the  strength and that gallantry - did you -  Jamie feel some responsibility and what  what you would like  people to take away from this film  thinking about your character and  thinking about Gloria Grahame as well  certainly enormous responsibility I mean  whenever you get to play someone who is  still around and is sharing their life  story with you and looks to you to do  them and their story justice of course  there's this heightened sense of I hope  I get this right I don't fail them you  know but I have to say Peter is is all  the pressure that was applied I applied  to myself because Peters so lovely and  so wonderful he was genuinely so excited  that I was playing him he really was  which then to be fair made me feel even  more nervous cuz I oh no he's even happy  that I'm doing it like he's actually  excited about it you know I want people  to go you know there are good decent  people out there you know these people  who you know Liverpool is a very  specific town they wear their heart on  their sleeve very much like um you know  the people of Boston it's a night it's  an identity based place they lead with  that and they take care of one another  and they love each other and Peter is  certainly one of those people you know  the movies about these very special  people who come into our lives and they  change us in our lives forever and you  know we're always trying to kind of  understand it and make sense of them and  in many ways they don't leave us you  know and and still now I've done many  interviews with Peter recently and to  this day he's still looking out for her  he's still protecting her in some ways  he is her guardian you know it's a love  story it's about its love in its purest  form you know it transcends so many  things I think this love story and I  just hope that people are affected by  that because that was the thing for him  you know in his life this is the most  meaningful one of the most meaningful  relationships he's ever had and I hope  we just captured a fraction of that you  know why don't you please thank Annette  Bening and Jay thank you very much

